00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.350 align:start position:0%
 
the justice secretary deputy prime  minister dominic rabb joins us on in the  studio good to see you this morning good  morning and i i i do promise we will get  to your brief in a few minutes time but  there's an awful lot around ukraine we  need to discuss we  we heard yesterday the us secretary of  defense lloyd austin saying that ukraine  believes it can win the war we've just  been speaking to our correspondent in  the donbass saying that forces there  believe they can win the war boris  johnson when he was in india struck a  note of caution suggesting that russia  might win is are we the voice of reason  in all of this or or perhaps is boris  johnson just parking some of the  boosterism when it comes to ukraine well  i think we want to show maximum  solidarity to ukraine but it's clear  that the conflict is in the balance  i've said since the first time i came on  sky news i didn't think it would be over  in weeks possibly not even in months so  we need the strategic stamina and  staying power and i think the prime  minister is very conscious of that so we  keep ramping up the pressure on putin  through the sanctions we keep supporting  as best we can with military hardware  and others support the ukrainians and we  do the most we can to deter  putin's behaviour i spoke to the icc  prosecutor yesterday we're rallying  countries to support the investigation  not just because we want to see justice  done for any war crimes we want to send  a deterrent message now to those  commanders in the field that if they  commit war crimes they'll end up behind  bars but just to pick up on something as  you yourself admit you've said before  not over in weeks and months you know  we could potentially be looking in into  the years certainly over 12 months in  terms of our involvement let's trust  your colleague the foreign secretaries  is making a really important speech  later on today in which she's going to  suggest that that two percent nato  spending target or on defense  should be the floor not the ceiling i  mean just in terms of the length of the  mission with our current spending how  sustainable is it for the uk and other  nations to keep sending arms with the  temple that we already have well that's  why we need the strategic stamina we're  one of the few countries that meets the  nato  target and i think there's long been  this argument and you've heard it from  successive american administrations that  some european countries need to step up  to the plate we agree with that  and at the same time we need to think  if this is going to last months possibly  years how we keep supporting at the  right pace the ukrainian resistance  which has been so sorry to interrupt but  it sounds like you kind of agree with  liz trust i mean when she refers to two  percent as as the floor not the ceiling  certainly suggests that liz trust is  minded to see a significant increase in  defence spending here  and that view is not universally held  around the cabinet table as i understand  it well no that we've seen a massive  increase in defense spending uh the  chancellor uh and the defense sector  worked very closely under the leadership  of the prime minister on that obviously  we've got a cost of living crisis we  need to cut our coats according to our  cloth overall but actually the direction  of travel has been to boost  and change the nature of our defenses  because of course then you can see this  in ukraine you can see this in terms of  russia as an adversary it's not just  conventional warfare we're seeing we've  also got cyber misinformation we need to  adapt to that that's exactly what the  chancellor and defense secretary have  done but the chancellor didn't do the  last opportunity that he did have did he  in the spring statement not by my record  all right  no no but we just had a spending review  we had a strategic uh uh defense uh  review uh an integrated review in  foreign policy that was absolutely the  right time to take a long-term forward  look and the chance that absolutely  along with the defense secretary  addressed this there just in terms  though of of what we are hearing from  the russians  by my measure i think this is the first  time that russia has made a direct  threat against the united kingdom  yesterday james he your colleague at the  mod said it was completely legitimate  for ukraine to seek out russian targets  to disrupt their logistics the russians  have responded saying well if you do  that if you keep encouraging them you  now become a legitimate target what do  you feel about that well the russian  statement is unlawful and what we're  doing is lawful international law is  very clear on this we're entitled all  states are entitled to provide military  support to any state exercising the  right of lawful defense against an  aggressive invasion and frankly if  russia starts threatening other  countries it only adds  further to their pariah status and will  only further the if you like solidarity  and the consensus of the international  community that they must be stopped  just before you can we've heard this  morning poland has had the gas cut off  we would assume therefore that  bulgaria will will follow quickly it  will quickly follow suit what can we do  to assist them because it does look like  poland is going to be in an awful lot of  trouble without that kind of energy well  i mean first of all we need to show  solidarity clearly what we are seeing is  the need to wean ourselves off uh  reliance on russia we've obviously got  very low reliance on gas from russia i  think it's around three percent uh  though some european countries in a  different position we have been warning  about this for a while but yes we'll  stand uh shoulder to shoulder with our  polish friends and allies they've said  that they can deal with this but of  course it will have a delete a very  damaging effect on russia as well  because it is becoming further and  further more and more not just a  political prior but an economic prior  and that will put the squeeze ultimately  on vladimir putin but one thing is true  and clear we cannot allow his bullying  behavior whether it's economic warfare  uh or its military warfare to succeed  can we just briefly then to return to  the to the mail on sunday article um  about angela raynor  we understand that the whips have been  taking a look into all of this i mean  here's the the the piece itself i mean i  don't i probably don't even need to ask  what your response is to the piece  itself i think i probably know but no  it's terrible and uh i i go up  occasionally against angela rainer in my  position as deputy prime minister uh  she's a formidable opponent i respect  her entirely and i can't stand this kind  of thing so has that have the whips  gotten anywhere closer to who was  responsible for all of this well i i i'd  need to speak to the chief whip about  this is something that he will be  looking at and of course it's very  difficult to find but what we're clear  is that we don't tolerate um we will not  tolerate any behavior and uh smear like  this and if we can find out we will take  action against it i i wonder what you  make of the the editor of the mail on  sunday's refusal to attend that meeting  he was invited by the speaker of the  house of commons  and the daily mail today being being  rather punchy i have to say about it no  mr speaker they're invoking the freedom  of the press i suppose the other view in  all of this says what was to be lost by  turning up  at the speaker's office and having a  conversation about this look i think uh  whether it's the speaker or the the  newspaper editors we all believe in two  things one the power and the importance  of free speech but secondly the  importance of not allowing that to be  abused and  and used to spread sexism in any shape  or form  i'm not going to second guess the the  the decision makers by decisions of  editors  it's his current common speaker should  the common speaker then have invited i  think it was a legitimate thing for  lindsay hall do to invite him and of  course it's the prerogative of any  editor to decide how they treat that  invitation um everyone yesterday at  cabinet was asked to come up with some  bright ideas for the cost of living  crisis i'm just wondering what yours  were sitting at at the head of the  justice department we look at um i don't  comment on the detail of what we discuss  in cabinet but i think there's a whole  range of things uh that we're looking at  first of all we've got a 22 billion  pound package to deal with the cost of  living but this prime minister and this  chance are going to be absolutely  assiduous and relentless in looking at  what more we can do we're providing  rebates on uh council tax on energy  bills we've got this pot of money that  local authorities can use to target the  most vulnerable and but we look as is  well known we discussed child care one  of the things there's lots of former  housing ministers around the cabinet  table we've discussed housing costs  these are all areas where we're never  going to stop we're going to continue  unflinchingly to look at what more we  can do and the reason being is we  understand the pressure that families up  and down the country around you  understand the kind of the knee jerk  response when you know the papers are  carrying stories that grant chaps big  bright idea was you know making annual  mot tests come you know biannually you  know torsten bell from the resolution  foundation says if this is the answer  then the government has lost the plot i  suppose the problem for you is if you  have all these individual policies and  you bring them together and you total  them up then eventually it may well be  that we see that the cost of living  crisis ameliorated somewhat but there's  no one big measure like the labour party  has the windfall tax which would see an  immediate kind of cash injection first  of all i don't agree in terms of the  characterization the conservative  policies the uc taper saves the relevant  families a thousand pounds a year um if  you look at what we've done on the  national living wage that saves a  thousand pounds a year for those  eligible full-time workers and frankly  if you look at labour's policy you asked  about it of a windfall tax that would  damage investment in energy supplies we  need and hike bills it's disastrous it's  not serious so what this shows is  they're coming up with frankly uh ill  thought through policies but we've got a  plan a concerted plan and i think that's  what voters want to see uh minister i am  nothing if not a man of your word so  let's talk about europe  and one has to say a fairly significant  announcement about the way we tackle  radicalization within our prisons tell  us more so look the the changing threat  from terrorism uh evolves all the time  but it's also evolving in prisons why  because we're locking up more terrorists  and extremists than ever before 200  convicted prisoners convicted of  terrorist offences another 200 prisoners  who've got terrorist links jonathan hall  has conducted this independent report we  accept it pretty much in full  and we got to do more to prevent  extremists tainting the well let me give  you two brief illustrations one we must  nip in the bud far earlier the kind of  coercive controlling behavior by  extremists which can lead to  radicalization and terrorism just to  give a concrete example of course we  want to respect the right for prisoners  to be able to eat halal prepare food  allow but they can't have no-go areas in  kitchens for those who don't respect or  comply with their rules secondly we've  got these separation centers which are  used to isolate the most dangerous  radicalizing recruiters of uh  of terrorists we need to probably make  greater use of that so i'm beefing up  the money to target those offenders who  need to be removed and finally we'll  need this bill of rights to replace the  human rights act to stop the legal  attrition that we're already starting to  see with terrorism extremists claiming a  right to socialize within prison when  what actually they want to do is  radicalize it sounds to me that that  last part is the most important part in  all of this if the kind of the mantra  that we hear from government about  activist lawyers is true and you would  certainly expect when you start bringing  in some of these changes for there to be  challenges all the way to the supreme  court well we've already had it we've  got a number of cases uh we've had to  it's cost us half a million pounds uh to  defend these cases we've not been taken  to court yet  but what i want to do is is put the  policies in place to protect the public  i think we have allowed understandable  uh cultural and religious sensitivities  to deter us as jonathan hall sets out  and of course what the bill of rights  will do is while respecting our  fundamental freedoms make sure we've got  the common sense balance and in this  context allowing prison governors to  take the action they need to retain the  security in the prison but also to  protect the public from these very  dangerous people great to have you in  the studio thank you

